Let \(f:\mathbb R^n	o\mathbb R\) have an \(L\)-Lipschitz gradient. From \(x_{k-1},x_k\), compute the accelerated trial \[y_k=x_k+ eta_k(x_k-x_{k-1}),\qquad z_k=y_k-L^{-1}
abla f(y_k),\] and the fallback \(g_k=x_k-L^{-1}
abla f(x_k)\). Set \(x_{k+1}\in\operatorname*{argmin}_{u\in\{z_k,g_k\}}f(u)\), breaking ties arbitrarily; if \(g_k\) is selected, reset the stored momentum. By the descent lemma, \(f(g_k)\le f(x_k)-\|
abla f(x_k)\|^2/(2L)\), so every accepted iterate satisfies \(f(x_{k+1})\le f(x_k)\).

Given a twice continuously differentiable function \(f:\mathbb R^n	o\mathbb R\), choose a fixed diagonal matrix \(D=\operatorname{diag}(d_1,\ldots,d_n)\) with \(d_i>0\), a step size \(\alpha>0\), and an initial point \(x_0\). Iterate \[x_{k+1}=x_k-\alpha D^{-1}
abla f(x_k)\] until a prescribed stopping test holds, then return the latest iterate. Equivalently, with \(x=D^{-1/2}u\) and \(g(u)=f(D^{-1/2}u)\), this is ordinary gradient descent \(u_{k+1}=u_k-\alpha
abla g(u_k)\). The transformed Hessian is \(
abla^2g(u)=D^{-1/2}
abla^2f(D^{-1/2}u)D^{-1/2}\), so the scaling can improve conditioning when this transformed spectrum is better clustered than the unscaled Hessian spectrum.

Given a twice continuously differentiable function \(f:\mathbb R^n	o\mathbb R\), choose a fixed symmetric positive-definite matrix \(M\), a step size \(\alpha>0\), and an initial point \(x_0\). Iterate \[x_{k+1}=x_k-\alpha M^{-1}
abla f(x_k)\] until a prescribed stopping test holds, then return the latest iterate. Under the change of variables \(x=M^{-1/2}u\), this is gradient descent on \(g(u)=f(M^{-1/2}u)\), whose Hessian is \(
abla^2g(u)=M^{-1/2}
abla^2f(M^{-1/2}u)M^{-1/2}\). A dense metric can improve spectral clustering, but its storage, factorization, and application or solve costs must be included in the computational tradeoff.

Let \(s_i>0\) and \(m\in\mathbb{R}^n\), define \(S=\operatorname{diag}(s_1,\ldots,s_n)\), and represent \(x=m+Sz\). For \(\widetilde f(z)=f(m+Sz)\), the chain rule gives \(
abla\widetilde f(z)=S^{\mathsf T}
abla f(m+Sz)\). Hence the normalized-coordinate step \(z_{k+1}=z_k-\alpha_k
abla\widetilde f(z_k)\) is exactly the original-coordinate step \(x_{k+1}=x_k-\alpha_kSS^{\mathsf T}
abla f(x_k)\). Choosing \(m_i\) and \(s_i\) from coordinate location and scale statistics therefore induces a fixed diagonal metric without changing the represented optimization problem.
